From 0c1fced960151efc8db97a174914d7f63e23fbb3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:14:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: keep a native Dolby Vision picture on the
 screen instead of drawing it again

Kodi draws the video at every refresh: while it is paused, and two or three
times per picture for film at 59.94 Hz with the refresh rate left as it is.
Native Dolby Vision output reconstructed, packed and committed the whole
3840x2160 picture each time (30 to 37 ms per FEL frame on the integrated
GPU of a Ryzen 9 7950X3D, which a 16.7 ms period cannot hold).

When the frame to draw is the picture already on the screen, it is kept
there: the same picture (its serial, now counted for the whole process so
that another renderer's cannot match), at the same place, with no GUI
element, overlay or capture on it or on the one on the screen, to be drawn
the way that one was (asked for directly or composed), with the last
presentation accepted, native output live and on, and nothing pending for
the next commit (properties Kodi set since, a modeset:
CDRMAtomic::HasPendingRequest()). Drawing it again would give the same
bytes: the renderer is stateless for it (no dithering, no peak detection,
no frame mixing), and the transport metadata of a picture of the same time
is the same payload with the same id (dvbridge_dv_metadata()); the render
test draws each picture once more after its commit and gets the same
hashes, float, packed and direct.

KeepDVBridgeFrame() paces the loop as the commit of that frame would:
after a frame presented the way that waits, until the next vblank of the
CRTC (drmWaitVBlank, by its index); after one presented a frame ahead,
until the flip of the frame before (its out-fence) or the refresh of a
picture kept before it, the refresh after that being kept as the flip of
this one, which the next commit, or EndDVBridge(), waits for. Where the
vblanks cannot be waited for, every frame is drawn in that native output.
Statistics count a kept picture as a frame shown (and say how many were
kept); the time between two flips of frames presented a frame ahead does
not count the refreshes of pictures kept between them as lateness; no GPU
timer query is made for a kept picture, and DVBridgeFrameShown() is told
of it as shown. <video><dvkeeprepeats>false</dvkeeprepeats></video> in
advancedsettings.xml draws every refresh as before.

Not run on a GPU or a display: the pacing has no test here.

On top of the fidelity step-down levels ("dvbridge: a fidelity step-down
level, forced by advancedsettings.xml for measurements", and
"windowing/gbm: lower the fidelity of native Dolby Vision only where the
GPU cannot keep up", which chooses a level for each picture format while
a video plays): a picture is kept only when it would be drawn at the
level that the one on the screen was drawn at (DVPicture::step), since
another level draws other bytes. The level of the frame is chosen before
that is asked, as for a frame that is drawn, so that the policy counts
the passes of the render loop as it did when every refresh was drawn. A
kept picture tells the policy nothing: no GPU time is taken of it, and
PresentRender() keeps it before a presentation of its own is told (the
flip of the frame before, which it waits for, is told as that frame's).
The flip of a frame presented a frame ahead is late, for the policy as
for the statistics, only by what it comes after the refreshes of the
pictures kept since the frame before. The GPU time on the Dolby Vision
page ("estuary: GPU time, headroom, ...") is that of the frames that
were drawn, as in the statistics of the log: its comments say so.

On top of the serializer of "dvbridge: send the Dolby Vision metadata as
CCM 001 and dovi_tool describe it": a picture of the same time is still
a repeat there (the same payload and metadata id, and no gap counted
towards a scene refresh), and a kept picture leaves the serializer where
drawing it again would, so the next new picture carries the same
metadata either way. The render test of the integrated tree draws each
picture once more after its commit and gets the same hashes.
